      "19304": {
        "comments": [
          {
            "count": 0,
            "is_markdown": false,
            "text": "GET_BINDING_CELL() in eval.c is how GETVAR and the other variable instructions find a binding cell the first time they touch a variable in a call, before it is in the per-call binding cache. Each such lookup calls R_findVarLocInFrame(), which calls findVarLocInFrame(), and for the frame of a function call that is a walk down a short unhashed list. Every call starts with an empty cache, so each argument and local a function uses costs those two calls once per call.\n\nTimings: binding-cell-frame-scan-bench.R, attached, prints ns per call for nine cases. On aarch64 (r90875), calls made from a loop inside a closure are 3 to 8% faster with both Apple clang 21 and gcc 15.2; calls from top level gain less, mostly within noise.\n\nProposed patch: attached as binding-cell-frame-scan.diff, against r90877. It does the walk in GET_BINDING_CELL() itself when the frame is unhashed; hashed frames go through R_findVarLocInFrame() as before.\n\nmake test-Specific test-Reg passes on macOS. The patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/472, with the full measurements.",
